Patch 2350 (video V1b, plans/video/v1b/README.md): the realtime admission limit accepts 50 ms of a 24 MHz clock.
iOS 26 userspace builds thread time constraints in 24 MHz ticks. CoreAudio's AMCP::Utility::configure_thread_for_realtime
(iOS 26.6.2 cache 0x1e210fd7c) passes period = constraint = the IO cycle and computation = clamp(cycle, 1200, 1200000).
On this kernel's 19.2 MHz timebase the stock 50 ms limit is 960000 ticks, so thread_policy_set refused every HAL IO thread
with a cycle of 960000 ticks or more. The 4096-frame media buffer is 2229115 ticks, so its IO stopped after 0 frames,
the AudioQueue start timed out (-66681) and Safari's MSE timebase stayed at 0 (ast226). max_rt_quantum becomes
max(50 ms, 1200000 ticks), 62.5 ms here and unchanged on 24 MHz hardware. sweetrtq=0 keeps the stock limit.
Kernel log lines: "SWEET RT: max rt computation" once at boot, then one per refused time constraint request (first 32)
and one per request only the allowance admits (first 8). Applies on top of 2330 (research/xnu-video-v0).

diff --git a/osfmk/kern/sched.h b/osfmk/kern/sched.h
